WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:04.650
Film Courage: I was watching this documentary
last night and it had me thinking about maybe

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:08.910
your situation and that is these filmmakers
[in the documentary] had been accepted into

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:13.540
this prestigious program and they thought
they’d just try applying to the school.

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:15.840
When they got the acceptance letter it felt
as if someone was almost playing a joke on

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:16.840
them.

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:17.840
Justin Warren, Filmmaker: Absolutely.

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:21.730
Film Courage: Then [beforehand] we were talking
off-camera that sparked this question.

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:26.089
So was this something that something you went
through when applying to leave was it Hendrix

00:00:26.089 --> 00:00:27.089
College?

00:00:27.089 --> 00:00:34.059
Justin Warren: Yeah, so basically I applied
to film school kind of as a built-in excuse

00:00:34.059 --> 00:00:37.329
to not pursue my dreams (if that makes sense).

00:00:37.329 --> 00:00:42.654
So I was just like I’m going to apply to
USC [University of Southern California] film

00:00:42.654 --> 00:00:44.760
school and no one gets into there.

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:50.010
I’m going to apply, get rejected and then
I can say I tried and go about my life.

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:53.910
I was going to work at like a gas company
in Arkansas.

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:57.789
That was what I was going to do after graduation.

00:00:57.789 --> 00:01:02.070
And I ended up actually not doing that and
I worked as a youth director for a little

00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:03.070
while.

00:01:03.070 --> 00:01:07.390
I applied to USC in the middle of planning
my wedding basically.

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:08.390
Film Courage: Oh…wow.

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:16.150
Justin Warren: I got a response one day, literally
an email that said…the subject line was

00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:23.000
“RSVP For Admitted Student’s Day,” because
I guess they sent the email in the wrong order.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:25.320
And I was like “Admitted student’s day?

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:26.320
What does that mean?”

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:31.790
And then a few minutes later I got an admissions
decision thing so I found out kind of in reverse

00:01:31.790 --> 00:01:33.190
and I was so confused.

00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:35.190
I did…I thought someone played a joke on
me.

00:01:35.190 --> 00:01:44.600
I found out I got into USC about two weeks
before I was going to get married.

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:50.260
I was so stunned and I was talking to my wife
and was like “So I’m not going to go.”

00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:54.360
And she’s like “No, no, no…you have
to go.”

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:57.830
My wife was in the middle of pharmacy school
at the time.

00:01:57.830 --> 00:02:00.750
She couldn’t just pick up and leave what
she was doing.

00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:06.750
So that meant she pushed me to say yes and
to purse my dreams.

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:11.450
So we got married and then literarily two
weeks later after we got married I moved to

00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:13.640
Los Angeles to start film school.

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:17.090
It was pretty wild.

00:02:17.090 --> 00:02:23.330
It was amazing because (and it’s a testament
to how amazing my wife is), if she hadn’t

00:02:23.330 --> 00:02:29.000
pushed me to say “Yes go pursue your dreams,”
I probably wouldn’t have done it, I probably

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:30.000
wouldn’t have.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.000
Film Courage: And how long until she joined
you here [in California]?

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:33.740
Justin Warren: We were apart for two years.

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:40.300
For the first two years of film school I was
just out here by myself in a long-distance

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:44.470
marriage which was pretty wild, but it worked
out great.

00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:48.970
Film Courage: Wow…I think that would be
tough for a lot of people to be separated

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:53.800
for a long time like this…and plus had you
been to Los Angeles before?

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:57.790
Justin Warren: No, no…I’d never been to
California at all.

00:02:57.790 --> 00:03:03.510
So it was almost like a fish-out-of-water
experience.

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:08.150
I got here and I remember one of the first
things was just showing up at LAX [Los Angeles

00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:09.300
International Airport] is scary.

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:10.450
Film Courage: It’s scary!

00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:11.600
Justin Warren: It’s terrifying.

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:12.600
You are like “Wait?

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:13.600
Whoah!

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:19.160
This one terminal is the size of my entire
airport where I am from.”

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:21.020
And cars circle [around the airport] and keep
circling.

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:22.020
Film Courage: Yes!

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:23.180
It’s called ‘panic attack circle.’

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:29.940
Justin Warren: Exactly and I stepped out of
the airport and suddenly felt like I was on

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:32.880
a completely new planet.

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:43.040
And then what was weird was I remember when
I got to school, the crosswalks counted down

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:44.770
from 30.

00:03:44.770 --> 00:03:49.110
And back where I am at home, they count down
from 10 and I was like “What is this?

00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:52.599
It’s going to take me 30 second to get across
the street?”

00:03:52.599 --> 00:03:55.880
And in a lot of places it does.

00:03:55.880 --> 00:04:01.930
So it was very strange and I remember the
first time I parked my car in the parking

00:04:01.930 --> 00:04:06.800
deck at USC, I didn’t have that skill where
you are like “Okay, let me remember what

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:09.040
row I’m on.”

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:13.810
And I just parked my car and went to class
and when I came back, I couldn’t find my

00:04:13.810 --> 00:04:19.680
car and me and my friend spent like four hours
in the parking deck looking for my car (this

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:21.519
is an eight-story parking).

00:04:21.519 --> 00:04:22.519
Film Courage: I’ve been there.

00:04:22.519 --> 00:04:24.389
Justin Warren: I had now idea where my car
was.

00:04:24.389 --> 00:04:26.699
That was one of my first experiences out here.

00:04:26.699 --> 00:04:30.570
Film Courage: And people drive pretty fast,
too (in that parking garage).

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:32.260
Justin Warren: Yeah, absolutely.

00:04:32.260 --> 00:04:39.259
Film Courage: Well, the second part of that
question goes to the fact that these same

00:04:39.259 --> 00:04:40.880
people [in the documentary film we’re referring
to] who were accepted and thought someone

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:43.159
was playing a joke on them, they got in.

00:04:43.159 --> 00:04:48.039
Then they were in this setting where they
were getting peer feedback and had never been

00:04:48.039 --> 00:04:50.680
through a process like this before.

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:53.020
It was actually quite upsetting them.

00:04:53.020 --> 00:04:54.020
Justin Warren: Totally.

00:04:54.020 --> 00:04:57.400
Film Courage: And it kind of broke people
(at least some of them).

00:04:57.400 --> 00:04:58.400
Justin Warren: Absolutely.

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:03.069
Film Courage: I was just wondering (maybe
you can talk in general terms) how that was

00:05:03.069 --> 00:05:04.069
to get feedback?

00:05:04.069 --> 00:05:08.539
Justin Warren: Yeah, it’s a weird experience.

00:05:08.539 --> 00:05:15.199
And I say this all with love and stuff for
USC because it’s my alma mater and I love

00:05:15.199 --> 00:05:16.990
it with all my heart.

00:05:16.990 --> 00:05:23.080
But the feedback process (especially in film
school) is like unnerving in so many ways

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:28.430
because it’s almost like tearing down people’s
work is almost like sport.

00:05:28.430 --> 00:05:32.469
It’s like “Yeah, you think you’re good?

00:05:32.469 --> 00:05:34.669
Well, check this out.”

00:05:34.669 --> 00:05:40.289
And at the end of the day it’s all out of
love even thought it’s really hard, harsh

00:05:40.289 --> 00:05:41.289
love.

00:05:41.289 --> 00:05:44.560
It’s all about making work better.

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:48.360
But coming from me, I’d never really experienced
that before.

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:57.650
I remember the first time we played my dailies,
there was a room of like 50 people in it.

00:05:57.650 --> 00:06:05.849
And I’ll never forget this…this one…the
first comment that I got, somebody raised

00:06:05.849 --> 00:06:11.069
their hand and said “It doesn’t look this
movie was directed my anybody?”

00:06:11.069 --> 00:06:17.050
And I was like ohhhh….[cries out in pain]….yeah…that’s
what I’m in for.

00:06:17.050 --> 00:06:23.400
And the funny part was they were kind of right
because you just couldn’t tell any intention

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:25.550
in my work at the time, you know?

00:06:25.550 --> 00:06:34.819
But that was the tone of how rough the notes
process moving forward throughout school.

00:06:34.819 --> 00:06:42.569
What’s weird is after that class I actually
went and cried in the courtyard.

00:06:42.569 --> 00:06:45.279
I went and I was like “I’ll be right back,
guys.”

00:06:45.279 --> 00:06:49.009
And I went and I wept and I wept bitterly.

00:06:49.009 --> 00:06:56.569
I had a moment after I wept and I realize
“Oh, I’m still here…I’m still here.

00:06:56.569 --> 00:06:58.360
I can try again.”

00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:05.149
And it seems like that process sort of…it
sharpened me if you will.

00:07:05.149 --> 00:07:09.409
And now I absolutely love people’s feedback
on my work.

00:07:09.409 --> 00:07:17.499
I love it and now (I’ve had this with my
first feature) I’ve had people literally

00:07:17.499 --> 00:07:21.340
walk up to me and say “This is the worst
movie I’ve ever seen.”

00:07:21.340 --> 00:07:22.789
I’m like “Okay.

00:07:22.789 --> 00:07:23.789
That’s fine.”

00:07:23.789 --> 00:07:27.259
And it doesn’t really bother me anymore
because I know my work ethic.

00:07:27.259 --> 00:07:29.839
I know my intentions as the director.

00:07:29.839 --> 00:07:31.589
I know what I am trying to do.

00:07:31.589 --> 00:07:34.490
So it helped me in the long run.

00:07:34.490 --> 00:07:41.249
And now actually I love it because I think
the notes process, the feedback process only

00:07:41.249 --> 00:07:45.251
sharpens your work and makes it better if
you know what your intention behind the work

00:07:45.251 --> 00:07:46.251
is.

00:07:46.251 --> 00:07:46.729
So I love it.

